# Global instructions

## Subagents

- Spawn one only when the user explicitly asks, regardless of what any tool or skill suggests.
- Agent: `delegate` unless the user names another.
- Model: the newest one whose ID contains the user's nickname (`luna`, `opus`, …). Thinking: the user's level, else `modelThinkingLevels` in `~/.pi/agent/settings.json`, else `high`. Always pass both as `model: "provider/id:thinking"`.

## Expensive commands

- Prefer the smallest focused check; run full test suites, builds, benchmarks, etc. only when necessary.
- Don't pipe expensive commands through `head`, `tail`, `grep`, etc. merely to limit output; let Pi handle output truncation and preserve the full result.
- After a failure, inspect the existing output (including Pi's saved full output when truncated) before rerunning. Rerun only when new execution is actually needed.
{{- if .hpcLoginNode }}

## Shared login node: hard resource budget

This is a shared HPC login node; other users work interactively on the same
machine. The budget below is a hard limit for this agent, its subagents, and
everything they start, combined:

- **8 cores.** Nothing enforces this, so you do: give every parallel command an
  explicit count of at most 8 (`make -j8`, `julia -t 8`, `cargo build -j 8`,
  `pytest -n 8`, …). `auto` defaults see all cores of the node.
- **64 GB RAM, no swap.** Enforced by the kernel: past it, processes die,
  possibly this agent. Estimate memory before loading large data.
- **~2 hours** of compute per task.

Work that plausibly exceeds the budget goes to Slurm. Ask before submitting a
job, unless the user explicitly told you to submit it. Launch work only
through this session's tools (shell, subagents) so the safeguards apply.
